 "map": "This last chapter is for revision before an exam or an interview. The table maps each kind of question to the chapter that teaches it, from simple filters and sorting, through grouping, joins and subqueries, to design, window functions, performance, transactions and security. The animation replays the logical order of a query, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY and LIMIT, because that single idea explains so many tricky questions. Revise one row of the table at a time, and use the Summary tab of each chapter as your quick notes. Think of a train map that shows every station on one page before a long journey.",
 "school": "School boards and first year courses ask short, exact questions. Show the names of class nine students in alphabetical order. Count the students in each class. Show courses whose average is above eighty, which needs HAVING because the condition uses an aggregate. Find students whose city starts with P using LIKE. Short answer questions come up every year too, such as the difference between WHERE and HAVING, between DELETE, DROP and TRUNCATE, and between a primary key and a unique key. The table on this page gives a model answer for each. Think of practising the past papers your teachers hand out before the board exam.",
 "interview": "These questions come up in interview after interview. Find the second highest salary, which is the largest salary below the largest, or the row with dense rank two. Find employees who earn more than their manager, which needs a self join of employees to employees. Find departments or customers with nothing linked, which needs NOT EXISTS or a LEFT JOIN with IS NULL, never NOT IN when NULLs are possible. Find the top earner in each department with RANK in a subquery. Interviewers love to hear you mention the traps, such as ties, NULLs and the boss with no manager. Think of a driving test where the examiner checks the same few manoeuvres every time.",
 "design": "University papers often give a story and ask for a schema. Here the college has clubs, advisers and students, and students join many clubs with a date and a role. So there are four tables, and memberships links students to clubs with a composite primary key. A check limits the role to three words, and a partial unique index makes sure each club has at most one president. To show third normal form, say that every cell holds one value, every column depends on the whole key, and nothing depends on a column that is not a key. Then write the query, with LEFT JOIN so clubs without a president still appear. Think of an architect presenting a plan and explaining every room.",
 "mock": "Here is a mock paper of hands on tasks, each checked by the real SQLite engine. They climb from easy to hard. Find products to reorder and sort them. Work out revenue per order with a three table join. List customers who never ordered with NOT EXISTS. Find each customer's latest order with a window function, and finally list the whole org chart with a recursive query. Give yourself about ten minutes per task, try without looking back, and only then press Show answer. If a task beats you, the hint names the chapter idea to revise. Think of a sports practice match played under real match rules.",
 "levels": "You have reached the end of the course. If you are a beginner, keep asking real questions of data you care about, like your expenses or a cricket table. If you are at college level, build a small app and write its queries by hand. To go deeper, read how SQLite and PostgreSQL work inside, and follow a university database course. For research, read the classic papers cited in each chapter, then new ones from the big database conferences. Keep the playground open and practise a little every day. Think of learning to swim, where a few lengths each day beat one long swim a month."
